{"id":"rdb-586f81","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"title":"Order-preserving key format (v2) with range and ORDER BY/LIMIT pushdown","estimate":8,"assignee":"Claude Code \u003cagent@macbook.local\u003e","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-09-28T13:31:25.028604Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-586f81","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"action":"created","artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T13:31:25.064689Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-ee17d5","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"relates_to","source_id":"rdb-ee17d5","target_id":"rdb-586f81"}],"description":"Follow-up from rdb-586f81. Rows are stored as JSON (`encodeRow`/`decodeRow`, with base64 cells). Profiling autocommit scans at 50k rows showed `decodeRow` (JSON unmarshal) at ~33% of query time, plus heavy allocation.\n\nrdb-586f81 makes scans stream and decode only returned rows, which reduces but doesn't remove the cost: full scans and large ranges still decode every row.\n\nDirection: a compact binary row format (length-prefixed or fixed-width cells per column type, reusing rdb-586f81's key codec where it fits), decoded without reflection. Like rdb-586f81, it's a storage format bump with no migration, acceptable while RepoDB is alpha. Measure full-scan and wide-range latency at 50k rows before and after.","estimate":5,"labels":["feature","performance"],"sort_order":"a8","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Faster row encoding (replace JSON row blobs) during the alpha format window"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T13:34:15.441713Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-acd36d","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"rdb-acd36d","target_id":"rdb-586f81"}],"description":"Follow-up from rdb-586f81, which makes the primary and secondary indexes `sql.OrderedIndex` with `Reversible = false`, because `prolly.Iterator` is forward-only. So `ORDER BY pk DESC LIMIT n` (for example, \"latest N rows\") still sorts the whole range.\n\nAdd a backward iterator (seek to an upper bound, walk right-to-left) to `common/prolly`, a reverse mode in rdb-586f81's streaming range iterator (merging overlay edits in descending order), and `Reversible = true`, so go-mysql-server can drop DESC sorts. Acceptance: `EXPLAIN` shows no Sort/TopN for `ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 20`, and at 50k rows its latency matches the ascending case.","estimate":3,"labels":["feature","performance"],"sort_order":"a9","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Reverse Prolly iteration for ORDER BY … DESC LIMIT pushdown"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T13:34:15.475197Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
