{"id":"rdb-ee17d5","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"status":"DOING","sort_order":"a0","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-09-28T15:37:40.063479Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-4c3ae3","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"Position RepoDB as a relational database that users can clone, query, branch, modify, inspect, merge, and synchronize through Git—whether or not the repository contains application code.\n\nThis roadmap begins after the current Tier 1 implementation and performance work establishes a credible supported workload envelope. It deliberately separates table-stakes adoption work from the differentiated version-control experience.\n\n## Product outcome\n\nRepoDB supports two complementary deployment modes:\n\n1. Embedded/local-first applications with explicit Git synchronization.\n2. A securely operated standalone MySQL-compatible server backed by the same cloneable, versioned data history.\n\n## Roadmap outcomes\n\n- Historical snapshots, diffs, named data branches, switching, merging, restore, and revert are first-class operations.\n- Standalone network service has secure defaults, authentication, TLS, authorization, and operational controls.\n- Common applications can rely on generated keys, referential integrity, and a tested compatibility profile.\n- Backup, recovery, compaction, format migration, and history maintenance make long-lived repositories operable.\n\n## Sequencing\n\n1. Finish Tier 1 performance/scalability work and publish the supported envelope.\n2. Establish snapshot-addressing and operational foundations.\n3. Deliver historical inspection and secure standalone access.\n4. Add data branching/merge workflows and broader application compatibility.\n5. Harden long-lived operation and publish a standalone deployment profile.\n\nChild stories remain BACKLOG until explicitly planned and specified.","labels":["epic"],"sort_order":"aA","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Roadmap: standalone Git-native relational database"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T15:44:44.501334Z","created_by":"codex-mcp-client/0.158.0 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-3a56dc","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"Make database history a first-class user workflow rather than an implementation detail.\n\nUsers can identify immutable snapshots, inspect history, query old state, compare versions, create and switch named data branches, merge branches, restore selected data, and revert committed changes. Operations must preserve uncommitted journal work or refuse safely; none may silently discard data.\n\nThis epic owns the differentiated workflow: clone → query → branch → modify → inspect → merge → sync.","labels":["epic"],"parent_id":"rdb-4c3ae3","sort_order":"aB","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Version-controlled data workflows"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T15:44:53.221336Z","created_by":"codex-mcp-client/0.158.0 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-04786e","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"Make RepoDB safe and operable as a standalone network service rather than a localhost-only development server.\n\nThe server must provide authenticated and encrypted connections, authorization, secure configuration, resource controls, and useful operational telemetry while preserving the embedded/local-only experience. Secure defaults must prevent accidental unauthenticated exposure.","labels":["epic"],"parent_id":"rdb-4c3ae3","sort_order":"aC","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Secure standalone server"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T15:44:53.247863Z","created_by":"codex-mcp-client/0.158.0 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-864fc6","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"Remove the highest-value SQL compatibility barriers for ordinary applications and frameworks.\n\nThis epic covers generated keys, referential integrity, distributed merge semantics for those features, and an evidence-based driver/ORM compatibility profile. Compatibility claims must be backed by tests through both the embedded API and MySQL wire protocol.","labels":["epic"],"parent_id":"rdb-4c3ae3","sort_order":"aD","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Application-ready SQL compatibility"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T15:44:53.268395Z","created_by":"codex-mcp-client/0.158.0 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-19616b","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"Make long-lived RepoDB repositories diagnosable, recoverable, maintainable, and upgradeable.\n\nThis epic covers corruption handling, consistent backup/restore, journal and Git-history retention, compaction/garbage collection, resilience testing, concurrent access validation, and storage-format migration. Maintenance operations must identify authoritative state and avoid silent data loss.","labels":["epic"],"parent_id":"rdb-4c3ae3","sort_order":"aE","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Operational durability and repository lifecycle"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T15:44:53.291021Z","created_by":"codex-mcp-client/0.158.0 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-5720c1","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"Make the MySQL-compatible server safe to expose beyond localhost.\n\n## Outcomes\n\n- Configure users and password credentials without placing plaintext secrets in repository history.\n- Support TLS certificates/keys and encrypted MySQL client connections.\n- Make bind address, TLS policy, and authentication policy explicit configuration.\n- Refuse accidental non-loopback unauthenticated exposure unless the operator chooses a clearly marked unsafe override.\n- Preserve a frictionless localhost development mode and embedded use.\n- Verify authentication success/failure, TLS negotiation, certificate errors, restart behavior, and standard MySQL client compatibility.\n\nAuthorization and role privileges are a separate child story; this story establishes connection identity and transport security.","parent_id":"rdb-04786e","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-09-28T15:45:09.813182Z","created_by":"codex-mcp-client/0.158.0 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-a2d28e","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"Support `AUTO_INCREMENT` integer columns as an application-compatibility feature without pretending a centralized sequence remains globally authoritative while clones are offline.\n\n## Outcomes\n\n- Parse and persist `AUTO_INCREMENT` metadata.\n- Allocate generated values transactionally for inserts that omit the column.\n- Return generated IDs consistently through embedded results and the MySQL wire protocol (`LAST_INSERT_ID()`/insert result where supported).\n- Persist allocation state across restart, checkpoint, branch switching, and recovery.\n- Define rollback, explicit-value insertion, overflow, and concurrent-writer behavior.\n- Define clone/branch merge semantics that never silently accept duplicate generated keys; collisions must be prevented or surfaced as explicit conflicts.\n- Document when globally unique application-assigned identifiers remain preferable for offline multi-writer datasets.","parent_id":"rdb-864fc6","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-09-28T15:45:09.854956Z","created_by":"codex-mcp-client/0.158.0 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-48af2f","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"Add SQL foreign-key constraints with explicit transaction and distributed-merge semantics.\n\n## Outcomes\n\n- Support inline and table-level `FOREIGN KEY ... REFERENCES` declarations and catalog persistence.\n- Enforce referential integrity on INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE.\n- Support RESTRICT/NO ACTION, CASCADE, and SET NULL with transactional atomicity.\n- Support `ALTER TABLE` add/drop foreign key and expose metadata to compatible clients.\n- Validate the merged catalog after three-way merge: parent deletion versus child creation, concurrent key changes, and cascades must never create silently invalid state.\n- Represent merge-created referential violations as inspectable, durable conflicts with actionable resolution, or reject publication before the live head advances.\n- Cover journal/native-Git persistence, restart, sync, and MySQL wire behavior.","parent_id":"rdb-864fc6","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-09-28T15:45:09.890606Z","created_by":"codex-mcp-client/0.158.0 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-686c51","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"parent_id":"rdb-864fc6","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-09-28T15:45:09.931984Z","created_by":"codex-mcp-client/0.158.0 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-cf94d8","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"parent_id":"rdb-19616b","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-09-28T15:45:09.971417Z","created_by":"codex-mcp-client/0.158.0 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
